{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>With the mirrors of the women who served.</b> The daughters of Israel had mirrors in their possession — mirrors they would look into when they adorned themselves. And even those they did not withhold from bringing for the donation of the Tabernacle, and Moses rejected them, because they are made for the evil inclination. The Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: Accept them, for these are more beloved to Me than everything, for through them the women raised up many hosts in Egypt. When their husbands were exhausted from crushing labor, they would go and bring them food and drink, and feed them and take the mirrors, and each one would see herself with her husband in the mirror, and entice him with words, saying: I am more beautiful than you, and through this would bring their husbands to desire and couple with them and conceive and give birth there, as it says: ״תַּחַת הַתַּפּוּחַ עוֹרַרְתִּיךָ״ (Song of Songs 8). And this is what is meant by בְּמַרְאֹת הַצֹּבְאֹת. And the laver was made from them — and it is for making peace between a man and his wife: to give waters from within it to one whose husband had jealousy toward her and she had secluded. And know for yourself that they were actual mirrors, for behold it says: ״וּנְחֹשֶׁת הַתְּנוּפָה שִׁבְעִים כִּכָּר״ etc., ״וַיַּעַשׂ בָּהּ״ etc. — and the laver and its base were not mentioned there. You learn from this that the bronze of the laver was not from the bronze of the wave-offering. Thus did Rabbi Tanchuma expound, and so Onkelos rendered: בְּמֶחְזְיָת נְשַׁיָא — and that is the translation of מַרְאוֹת, מירוריי\"ש בְּלַעַ\"ז. And likewise we find: ״וְהַגִּלְיוֹנִים״ (Isaiah 3) — translated as מֶחְזְיָתָא." "<b>Who served.</b> To bring their donation."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 38, :notes {0 "the la'az is Old French miroirs, 'mirrors'"}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:56:54.902665664Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}